173006.394: [17:30:06][root@172-16-208-2 ~]# crontab -l
173006.415: [17:30:06]# Edit this file to introduce tasks to be run by cron.
173006.416: [17:30:06]# 
173006.416: [17:30:06]# Each task to run has to be defined through a single line
173006.416: [17:30:06]# indicating with different fields when the task will be run
173006.416: [17:30:06]# and what command to run for the task
173006.416: [17:30:06]# 
173006.416: [17:30:06]# To define the time you can provide concrete values for
173006.416: [17:30:06]# minute (m), hour (h), day of month (dom), month (mon),
173006.416: [17:30:06]# and day of week (dow) or use '*' in these fields (for 'any').
173006.416: [17:30:06]# 
173006.416: [17:30:06]# Notice that tasks will be started based on the cron's system
173006.416: [17:30:06]# daemon's notion of time and timezones.
173006.416: [17:30:06]# 
173006.416: [17:30:06]# Output of the crontab jobs (including errors) is sent through
173006.416: [17:30:06]# email to the user the crontab file belongs to (unless redirected).
173006.416: [17:30:06]# 
173006.416: [17:30:06]# For example, you can run a backup of all your user accounts
173006.416: [17:30:06]# at 5 a.m every week with:
173006.416: [17:30:06]# 0 5 * * 1 tar -zcf /var/backups/home.tgz /home/
173006.416: [17:30:06]# 
173006.416: [17:30:06]# For more information see the manual pages of crontab(5) and cron(8)
173006.416: [17:30:06]# 
173006.417: [17:30:06]# m h  dom mon dow   command
